 ## Overview
-Non-Parametric Transfer Function Generation is a clustering method for the 2D histogram (conventional or abstracted) using variable kernel density estimation to group data based on neighborhood information [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from fixed-bandwidth density methods by adapting the kernel width to local data density, ensuring accurate estimation in sparse regions without assuming a specific number of clusters [S1, unofficial].
+Non-Parametric Transfer Function Generation is a clustering method for 2D histograms (conventional or abstracted) that groups data based on neighborhood information using variable kernel density estimation [S1, unofficial]. It differs from discrete histogram-based clustering by operating on continuous density estimates, thereby avoiding spurious peaks and preserving spatial relationships of neighboring data points [S1, unofficial]. Unlike frequency binning methods that consider only density magnitude, this method incorporates position in feature space via adaptive kernel widths, preventing disjoint segments for the same density value [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input:** A set of $N$ samples $\{X_1, X_2, \dots, X_N\}$ in a 2D feature space (e.g., a 2D histogram of volume data values and gradients) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Output:** A probability density estimate $\hat{f}_h(x)$ for any location $x$ in the feature space, which identifies clusters as local maxima or high-density regions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** A set of $N$ data points $\{X_1, X_2, ..., X_N\}$ representing voxels in a 2D feature space (e.g., Gray-Gradient Mode Histogram).
